{
  "payloads": [
    {
      "text": "### Chapter 5 Excerpt: Adaptive Policy Envelopes in Practice - The Netherlands Coastal Flood Management Case\n\n**Title:** Adaptive Policy Envelopes in Practice: The Netherlands Coastal Flood Management Case\n\nThe Netherlands provides an illustrative example for applying the Adaptive Policy Envelope Design (APED) framework. It operates under the Water Act of 2009, mandating flood protection standards expressed as annual exceedance probabilities, which vary based on the population density of regions. For example, densely populated western polders have an exceedance probability of 1 in 10,000 per year, whereas less exposed agricultural zones have a probability of 1 in 300 per year. This regulatory framework establishes a well-defined information set \\(I(t)\\) and an outcome space \\(S\\) that includes crucial variables—such as sea level, storm surge frequency, subsidence rates, and dike failure probabilities—all extensively monitored globally.\n\n#### Application of the Framework\n\nThe case focuses on the IJsselmeer closure decision, addressing whether, when, and how to construct a secondary tidal barrier at the mouth of the IJssel estuary to complement the existing Afsluitdijk. The decision involves a policy envelope structure, wherein the government authority (Rijkswaterstaat) must commit to either construction, deferral, or abandonment of the project. The optimal choice relies on uncertain variables affecting future conditions, including sea-level rise, population density in protected zones, and the cost trajectories of alternative barrier technologies.\n\nTo formulate the decision-making under uncertainty, historical data from 1990 to 2019 and climate projections from the KNMI were utilized to estimate prior distributions over sea-level rise paths. The Bayesian updating rule is parameterized as follows:\n\n- Let \\(r(t)\\) denote the observed mean sea level relative to the NAP datum at year \\(t\\).\n- The probability assignments over four KNMI scenarios (G, GL, W, WL) are revised based on the likelihood of the observed sequence \\(r(t)\\) under each scenario. The innovation of the APED framework is in embedding this Bayesian filter directly into the policy commitment: the decision rule specifies beforehand which portfolio variant will be selected based on the scenario receiving the highest posterior probability at the mandatory five-year review point.\n\n#### Findings\n\nThe analysis leads to two significant outcomes:\n\n1. **Optimal Initial Portfolio**: The optimal portfolio (E(P)) that maximizes expected welfare across scenarios while ensuring downside robustness defers construction commitment for twelve to eighteen years, focusing on site preparation and monitoring technology. This conclusion remains consistent across various prior specifications.\n\n2. **Value of Embedded Updating Rule**: The advantage of implementing a Bayesian updating mechanism—where the initial decision isn't treated as fixed—yields substantial benefits. It translates to approximately €2.3 billion in expected welfare improvements over a fifty-year planning horizon compared to a conventional fixed-commitment approach. The rationale is clear: early investments in monitoring contribute to reduced posterior uncertainty, enabling the authority to align construction timing with emerging evidence rather than hedging against all potential scenarios simultaneously.\n\n### Page Range:\n**Pages:** 163-214  \n**Word Count:** 420 words\n\nThis example illustrates the application of the APED framework to real-world policy decisions regarding climate adaptation and can serve as a valuable teaching tool in your seminar. If you need more details or a different aspect of the book, feel free to ask!",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
  ],
